LESSONS_S7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Sales Invoice: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Sales Invoice, payments, credit notes, receivables, and collections in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Sales Invoice field walk; use the correct creation path for each billing basis; state what submission posts; record and allocate Payment Entries including advances; issue credit notes through the return tie; run the receivables and statement cycle; and operate the four-gear collections procedure.</p><p><b>What a Sales Invoice is.</b> The Sales Invoice is the accounting event of the sale: it posts the revenue, the output VAT, and the receivable. On the document timeline (module 6, Chapter 1) it is the third posting — the order reserved, the delivery deducted stock and posted cost, and the invoice now claims the money. From submission, the customer owes the invoiced amount on the due date the terms compute, and the receivables machinery tracks it until it is paid, credited, or escalated.</p><p><b>Position and dependencies.</b> The invoice draws on everything the track has built:</p><p>1. <b>The customer record</b> (module 1) — the payer, the terms template, the TIN the e-invoicing regime requires.<br>2. <b>The pricing and tax layer</b> (modules 2, 6) — rates from the order's agreement; tax computed by configuration at the channel's posture.<br>3. <b>The order and its schedule</b> (module 4) — what is billable, per the payment schedule and the delivered state.<br>4. <b>The deliveries</b> (module 5) — the %delivered figures and the delivered-not-billed list the invoicing run clears.</p><p><b>The invoicing run.</b> Invoicing operates on a cadence per the policy — the standing pattern is a scheduled run (daily or twice-weekly per volume) that works the delivered-not-billed list and the due milestones to zero, plus immediate invoicing where the flow requires it (counter sales, cash-and-carry). A sale delivered but unbilled is performance without a claim; the run's discipline keeps that interval short and measured.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The invoicing role prepares and submits invoices per the matrix; the accounts function owns the receivable, the allocation of money, and the statements; collections operate per the written procedure (Chapter 8); and the standing rule: <b>every invoice states what the order agreed and the delivery performed — billed from the documents, due by the template, and tracked to its settlement.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the field walk. Chapter 3: creation paths. Chapter 4: what submission posts. Chapter 5: Payment Entries and allocation. Chapter 6: credit notes. Chapter 7: receivables and statements. Chapter 8: collections. Chapter 9: worked example. Chapter 10: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Sales Invoice doctype", "12", "<p>Create the Sales Invoice from its source document (Chapter 3 covers the paths). Complete or verify the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Customer and header.</b> The customer carries from the source with the terms template, currency, and billing address. The TIN presence check applies where e-invoicing operates — a customer record missing its TIN is corrected at the record (module 1's gate requirement) before the invoice submits.</p><p><b>2. Posting Date and Due Date.</b> The posting date is the invoice date — the date the revenue and receivable post, set honestly per the standing date rule. The due date computes from the payment terms template (module 1, Chapter 5): the schedule rows generate per the template's portions and intervals. Due dates are never typed over — the receivables aging computes from them, and a typed date detaches the invoice from the terms decision.</p><p><b>3. The items table.</b> Rows draw from the source document:</p><p>1. <b>Quantities</b> — the billed quantities per the basis: delivered quantities when billing from deliveries; the milestone's portion when billing the schedule.<br>2. <b>Rates</b> — carried from the order's agreement; the invoice does not renegotiate. A rate correction at invoice time is an order amendment first (module 4's taxonomy), then a re-draw.<br>3. <b>References</b> — the Sales Order and Delivery Note links per row: the references connect the claim to the agreement and the performance, and every reconciliation and dispute works through them.</p><p><b>4. Taxes.</b> The template and item treatments compute per module 6 — read, not edited. The item-wise breakup on a mixed invoice is verified against the ruling table on the sample cadence.</p><p><b>5. The payment schedule section.</b> On milestone orders, the invoice's schedule rows reflect the portion being claimed (Chapter 3.3); on standing-terms invoices, the single row at the template's interval.</p><p><b>6. Advance allocation.</b> Where advances exist against the order (module 4, Chapter 6), the allocation section applies them against this invoice — Chapter 5 covers the mechanics. The rule at entry: the allocation is part of preparing the invoice, not a later cleanup.</p><p><b>7. Save, review, submit.</b> Draft review per the value threshold; submission posts the accounting event (Chapter 4) and, under the e-invoicing regime, transmits the document (module 6, Chapter 2's rules: the transmitted record is permanent; corrections follow the credit-note lane).</p><p><b>Verification per invoice.</b> The customer and terms correct; the posting date honest; quantities per the basis; rates per the agreement; references present; taxes read back to template and treatment; advances allocated; then submit on the run's cadence.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Creation paths and billing bases", "12", "<p>An invoice is created from its billing basis. This chapter is the three paths and when each applies.</p><p><b>1. From the Delivery Note — billing performance.</b> The standard trade path: Create &gt; Sales Invoice from the Delivery Note (or from several notes consolidated per the policy). The invoice draws the delivered rows and quantities; %billed advances against the order; the delivered-not-billed list clears. This path bills exactly what shipped, and the row references carry both documents.</p><p><b>2. From the Sales Order — billing the schedule.</b> The milestone path: Create &gt; Sales Invoice from the order, drawing the portion the payment schedule makes due — the advance portion at order (where the terms bill the advance as an invoice per the site's configuration), the delivery portion when its wave ships, the retention at completion. The invoice's rows and schedule section state the portion claimed; the order's %billed tracks the cumulative claim against the total. Milestone billing follows the schedule as written — the module 4 milestone read exists to catch portions that fell between runs.</p><p><b>3. Direct invoices — policy-limited.</b> Invoices without a source document are confined to the cases the policy names (counter sales through the POS flow, defined service charges): each carries its own complete data and its policy reference. A direct invoice for what should have been an order-and-delivery flow bypasses reservation, fulfilment tracking, and the document chain — the monthly invoice sample (Chapter 7) lists direct invoices against the policy's cases.</p><p><b>4. Choosing the path.</b> The decision is mechanical: bill performance from the Delivery Note; bill the schedule from the Sales Order; bill nothing directly except the policy's named cases. Mixed situations resolve by their parts — the delivered wave bills from its notes while the milestone structure governs how much of the value is claimable now; the site's configuration (deduct advances, percentage billing) implements the arithmetic, and the order file shows the claim's build-up.</p><p><b>5. Consolidation and splitting.</b> Per the policy: multiple deliveries to one customer may consolidate onto one invoice within a period (fewer documents for the customer's payables to process); one delivery never splits across invoices without a schedule reason (partial claims create allocation complexity with no operational gain). Consolidated invoices keep per-row references so each line traces to its delivery.</p><p><b>6. The run's worklist.</b> Each invoicing run works two lists to zero: the delivered-not-billed list (path 1) and the due-milestones list (path 2). Both lists come from the track's standing reads; the run's output — invoices submitted, exceptions carried with reasons — files with the run per the one-lookup standard.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — What submission posts", "12", "<p>Submission converts the verified draft into the accounting event. This chapter states each posting and its consequence.</p><p><b>1. The receivable.</b> The customer's account (the receivables control through the customer ledger) debits for the gross total: the customer now owes the invoice on its due date. The customer's outstanding balance — the figure the credit limit checks against (module 1) — increases accordingly.</p><p><b>2. The revenue.</b> The income accounts credit per the item defaults (module 2's sales defaults, per the accountant's design): the sale's revenue is recognised on the posting date. Where cost posted at delivery (module 5), the margin for the period reads from the two documents together — cost at despatch, revenue at invoice — and the delivered-not-billed interval is the timing gap between them.</p><p><b>3. The tax.</b> The VAT rows credit the output liability account per the template (module 6): the period's return accumulates. Under e-invoicing, submission also transmits the document per the compliance configuration — the fiscal record and the ledger record are created by the same act, and both are permanent.</p><p><b>4. The order update.</b> %billed advances on the linked order; the milestone rows mark as claimed; the order's status moves per the vocabulary when billing completes.</p><p><b>5. Corrections after submission.</b> The lanes, matching what the error is:</p><p>1. <b>The invoice claims what did not happen</b> (wrong quantities, wrong customer, duplicate): under e-invoicing the correction is a credit note reversing the claim plus a correct re-invoice — the transmitted record is never deleted. Outside the regime, same-day cancellation with reason is permitted per the document rules; the credit-note lane remains the standard where any doubt exists.<br>2. <b>The claim is right but the money side changes</b> (returned goods, agreed price adjustment after the fact): the credit note lane (Chapter 6), sized to the change, referenced to the invoice.<br>3. <b>A field error with no accounting effect</b> (a reference, a description): the administrative note per the document rules.</p><p><b>6. The GL tie.</b> The invoice's postings participate in the accounts function's controls: the receivables control account reconciles to the customer ledger, the revenue accounts reconcile to the sales register (module 6, Chapter 7), and the VAT accumulation reconciles to the return. An invoicing function that keeps its documents clean — honest dates, template-computed tax, referenced rows — is what makes those reconciliations arithmetic rather than investigation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Payment Entries and allocation", "12", "<p>Money arrives and must land on the right claims. This chapter is the Payment Entry and the allocation discipline.</p><p><b>1. The Payment Entry.</b> Open Payment Entry (awesome bar) &gt; New (or Create &gt; Payment from the invoice): the party, the payment type (Receive), the amounts, the bank or cash account the money entered, the date, and the reference (the customer's transfer reference or instrument number). The entry records that money arrived; the allocation section records what it pays.</p><p><b>2. Allocation.</b> The allocation table lists the customer's open invoices; the received amount allocates against them:</p><p>1. <b>Per the customer's remittance advice</b> — the customer states which invoices the payment covers; the allocation follows their statement, because the customer's ledger and the business's must agree invoice-by-invoice.<br>2. <b>Partial payments</b> — an invoice may allocate partially; the balance remains outstanding at its due date and ages accordingly.<br>3. <b>Unallocated amounts</b> — money received without identifiable invoices posts as unallocated (advance) against the customer and is resolved through the weekly unallocated read: the remittance chased, the allocation completed. A ledger carrying old unallocated amounts and old open invoices side by side is one reconciliation behind on every statement it sends.</p><p><b>3. Advance allocation.</b> The order advance (module 4, Chapter 6) allocates when its invoices raise:</p><p>1. The milestone invoice prepares with its allocation section drawing the order's advance.<br>2. The advance applies per the schedule's arithmetic — the advance portion offsets the claims it funded, in order, until consumed.<br>3. The customer's statement then reads coherently: the order, the advance received, the invoices, the allocations, the balances due at their dates.</p><p><b>4. Payment discipline.</b></p><p>1. <b>Same-day recording</b> — payments record on the day they are identified in the bank, at the amount received; the receivables position is only as current as the last recorded payment.<br>2. <b>No netting by hand</b> — deductions the customer takes (a disputed line, a claimed rebate) are not absorbed by under-allocating silently: the short-pay records as a partial allocation and the difference routes to its lane — the credit note if the deduction is agreed, the dispute procedure if not.<br>3. <b>Reconciliation</b> — the bank reconciliation (the Accounts track) confirms that recorded payments match bank movements; the selling-side obligation is entries that are complete, dated, and referenced.</p><p><b>5. The allocation reads.</b> Weekly: unallocated receipts by age, worked to zero; invoices with partial allocations, confirmed against remittances. Monthly: the allocation sample — payments traced from bank line to entry to invoice — riding the accounts function's close.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Credit notes", "12", "<p>The claim reduces when goods return or terms adjust. This chapter is the credit note lane.</p><p><b>1. The credit note.</b> A Sales Invoice marked as a return (Is Return), created against the original invoice so the reference carries. It posts the reverse of Chapter 4: the receivable credits (the customer owes less), revenue debits, and the VAT output reverses for the credited value — the return's tax leaves the period's accumulation, which is what keeps the VAT return aligned with the commercial reality.</p><p><b>2. The return tie.</b> The standing case is module 5's return lane completing its money side:</p><p>1. The return Delivery Note recorded what came back (quantities, identities, condition).<br>2. The credit note issues against the original invoice for the returned quantities at their invoiced rates — the two documents referenced to each other.<br>3. The rule from module 5 restated: <b>the stock document records what came back; the credit document records what is owed for it</b> — the credit note never issues without its physical return where goods are involved, and the return read lists credit notes against their return documents.</p><p><b>3. Non-return credits.</b> Price adjustments agreed after invoicing (a billing error, a negotiated settlement of a dispute) issue as credit notes without stock rows — value-only credits with the reason and the approval per the ladder band the adjustment belongs to. These are the exception lane, listed monthly with approvers, because a value credit is a price change after the fact and carries the same governance as any other price movement.</p><p><b>4. Credit or refund.</b> The credit note creates a balance in the customer's favour. Its settlement:</p><p>1. <b>Allocation against open or future invoices</b> — the standard case for trading relationships: the credit offsets what the customer owes or will owe.<br>2. <b>Refund</b> — a Payment Entry (Pay) returning money, per the policy's authorisation: refunds move cash and carry the tighter approval.<br>3. The choice records with the credit; a credit note left unsettled and unallocated ages on the ledger like any open item and appears in the weekly read.</p><p><b>5. E-invoicing and credit notes.</b> Under the regime, credit notes transmit like invoices and reference the fiscal record they adjust. The operational consequences: the credit note's data completeness matters at submission (the reference to the original, the reason coding per the configuration), and the invoice-plus-credit pair is the permanent public record of the correction — the reason the standing rule prefers the credit lane even where cancellation is technically available.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Receivables and statements", "12", "<p>Open invoices age until they are paid. This chapter is the receivables view and the statement cycle.</p><p><b>1. The receivables aging.</b> The Accounts Receivable report: open invoices per customer, bucketed by age against due date (current, then the aging buckets per the site's configuration). The figures the report depends on are the ones this track disciplined: due dates from templates (module 1), invoices from documents (Chapters 2-3), payments allocated (Chapter 5), credits settled (Chapter 6). The aging is read weekly by the collections function and monthly by the owner with the receivables summary.</p><p><b>2. Reading the aging.</b> The standing reads on the report:</p><p>1. <b>The overdue concentration</b> — overdue value by customer, largest first: the collections worklist (Chapter 8).<br>2. <b>The bucket drift</b> — value moving rightward across buckets between reads: an account drifting from current to 30-plus is engaged at the drift, not at the write-off conversation.<br>3. <b>The integrity anomalies</b> — negative lines (unallocated credits or payments sitting beside open invoices), old partial balances, disputed lines flagged per the dispute procedure: each routes to its lane — allocation, credit settlement, or the dispute file.</p><p><b>3. The statement cycle.</b> The Statement of Accounts per customer (module 1, Chapter 5) sends on the monthly cadence to the named accounts payable contact, and on demand at collection conversations:</p><p>1. The statement lists invoices, credits, payments, allocations, and the balance by due date — the customer's copy of the ledger.<br>2. Statement disputes are findings: a customer's ledger disagreeing with the statement traces through the references (invoice to delivery to order) and resolves at the document that differs; recurring disagreement with one customer indicates an allocation or documentation gap on one side, located through the monthly reconciliation of that account.<br>3. The statement is the collections procedure's second gear (Chapter 8); its regularity is what makes the overdue conversation a follow-up rather than a surprise.</p><p><b>4. Dunning configuration.</b> Where the site uses the system's reminder machinery, the reminder templates and cadence are configured per the collections procedure — the letters state facts from the ledger (invoices, dates, amounts) in the firm's standard wording, and every sent reminder records against the customer. Automation serves the procedure; the gears and their decisions remain Chapter 8's owned steps.</p><p><b>5. The receivables summary — monthly.</b> The owner's view: total receivables, overdue value and percentage, DSO or collection-days per the accountant's measure, the top overdue accounts with their gear status, credits outstanding, unallocated cash. One page, from the reads above, filed with the month's commercial review (module 6, Chapter 7).</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Collections: the four-gear procedure", "13", "<p>Collections run as a written procedure with four gears, each with its trigger, its action, and its record. Every contact records against the customer with date, person, and outcome — the collections file is part of the account's record.</p><p><b>1. Gear one — the pre-due contact.</b> Trigger: an invoice above the policy's value threshold reaches <b>7 days before due</b>. Action: the courtesy contact to the accounts payable contact — the invoice is with them, complete, approved for payment, and any query is surfaced now. Purpose: queries and missing-document problems are resolved before the due date instead of becoming the reason for lateness after it. The pre-due read lists the week's qualifying invoices; the contacts record.</p><p><b>2. Gear two — the statement at due.</b> Trigger: the due date passes without payment. Action: the statement (Chapter 7) with the due items marked, sent to the named contact with the standard covering note. Purpose: the formal record that the balance is due, in the customer's payables process, referenced for every later step.</p><p><b>3. Gear three — the overdue ladder.</b> Trigger: the policy's overdue intervals. Action, stepped:</p><p>1. <b>The first reminder</b> at the first interval — the reminder letter with the ledger facts; the call to the contact; the outcome recorded (a promise-to-pay date enters the file and is followed up on that date).<br>2. <b>The escalated reminder</b> at the second interval — the firmer standard letter; the conversation moves up a level on both sides per the procedure.<br>3. <b>The credit hold</b> at the policy's trigger — new orders for the account block per module 1's machinery (the limit and hold settings), the customer informed per the standard notice. A hold is a documented decision by the named role, and its purpose is stated plainly: stopping the exposure growing while the balance resolves.</p><p><b>4. Gear four — the resolution decision.</b> Trigger: the ladder exhausts or the account disputes. Action: the owner's decision from the file — the negotiated settlement (a payment plan with dates, recorded and followed like promises), the dispute resolution through the document references, or the escalation to formal recovery per the policy. Write-offs, where reached, are the accountant's controlled entries with the owner's approval, and the account's credit posture resets per the ladder.</p><p><b>5. The collections reads.</b> Weekly: the gear worklist — accounts by gear with next actions and promise dates due. Monthly: gear outcomes — amounts collected by gear, promises kept and broken, holds imposed and lifted; the figures file with the receivables summary. The procedure's measure is simple: overdue value falling, and no account reaching gear three whose gear-one contact was never made.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Worked example: invoicing the BizRetail Ltd order", "13", "<p>This chapter runs the module through the enterprise order modules 3-5 built: BizRetail Ltd, the 12-branch rollout, the 50/40/10 schedule, wave one delivered to five branches. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the advance invoice (Chapters 3, 5).</b> Per the schedule and the site's configuration, the <b>50% advance</b> portion was claimed at order and funded before the work queue took the order (module 4). The Payment Entry recorded the advance against the order; the advance sits identified on BizRetail Ltd's ledger awaiting allocation.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the delivery-portion invoice (Chapter 3).</b> Wave one submitted (module 5): five branches, fifteen units, acknowledged. The invoicing run draws the wave from the delivered-not-billed list and bills the <b>40% delivery portion</b> for the delivered branches from the wave's Delivery Notes — quantities per the notes, rates per the order (the 4% terminal concession intact through to the invoice rows), references carrying order and delivery per row. Taxes compute at the trade posture: net, VAT at 7.5%, gross — the arithmetic BizRetail Ltd's procurement office reconciles line by line.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the allocation (Chapter 5).</b> The invoice prepares with its advance allocation: the advance applies against the delivery-portion claim per the schedule's arithmetic, and the invoice's balance due computes at the customer's terms. BizRetail Ltd's statement now reads in order: the order reference, the advance received, the wave invoice, the allocation, the balance at its due date.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the return's money side (Chapter 6).</b> The damaged printer (module 5's return) was resolved by replacement, not credit — the return and replacement documents cross-reference, and no credit note issues because no value changed hands. The example states the rule by contrast: had the resolution been a return for credit, the credit note would have issued against the wave invoice for one printer at its invoiced rate, referenced to the return Delivery Note.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the gears in use (Chapters 7-8).</b> The wave invoice crosses the value threshold, so gear one runs at <b>7 days before due</b>: the pre-due contact confirms the invoice is in BizRetail Ltd's payment cycle with no queries — recorded. Payment arrives by transfer at terms; the Payment Entry records same-day and allocates per the remittance advice; the aging shows the account current. %billed advances on the order; waves two and three will repeat the cycle, and the <b>10% retention</b> bills at completion per the milestone read.</p><p><b>The file.</b> The order file now carries the full chain: quotation versions, the order and its amendment, five Delivery Notes and acknowledgments, the return pair, the advance entry, the wave invoice with its allocation, and the collections contacts — the account answerable end to end from one file, per the standing standard.</p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in invoicing and collections, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the typed due date.</b> Symptom: due dates entered by hand; the aging computing from improvised dates; terms decisions bypassed. Correction: templates re-applied; the aging re-read. Prevention: due dates only from the terms template, per module 1.</p><p><b>Error 2 — billing beside the documents.</b> Symptom: direct invoices for what should be order-and-delivery flows; quantities disagreeing with despatches; claims with no references. Correction: the chain reconstructed; the invoice corrected through the credit lane. Prevention: the three-path rule and the monthly direct-invoice sample.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the unallocated ledger.</b> Symptom: payments and credits sitting unallocated beside open invoices; statements the customer disputes; the aging overstating exposure. Correction: the weekly unallocated read worked to zero against remittances. Prevention: allocation as part of recording, and advances allocated at each milestone invoice.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the silent short-pay.</b> Symptom: customer deductions absorbed by under-allocation; disputes and rebates invisible in the record. Correction: the differences surfaced and routed — credit note or dispute lane. Prevention: partial allocations recorded as partial, with the difference's lane decided at recording.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the credit note without a return.</b> Symptom: goods-related credits issued with no return document; stock and money records disagreeing about what came back. Correction: the return documented or the credit reversed. Prevention: the two-document rule and the return read listing credits against returns.</p><p><b>Error 6 — collections by memory.</b> Symptom: contacts unrecorded; promises unfollowed; accounts reaching deep overdue with no gear history. Correction: the file reconstructed; the gears applied from the current state. Prevention: every contact recorded, the weekly gear worklist, and the gear-one discipline on qualifying invoices.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the invoice walk with honest dates, template due dates, and per-row references; choose the correct creation path and run the worklists to zero; state the four postings and the three correction lanes under the e-invoicing rules; record and allocate payments including advances and short-pays; operate the credit lane with the return tie and the credit-or-refund decision; read the aging and run the statement cycle; and run the four gears with their triggers and records.</p><p><b>Module rule 7: the invoice claims what the documents support — billed from the delivery or the schedule, due by the template, transmitted as the permanent record, settled by allocated payments and referenced credits, and collected by the four-gear procedure with every contact recorded.</b> Module 8 covers bundles, blanket orders, and the advanced selling instruments.</p>"),
]

